@use-modules[(math-derivations commands) (grove math pipeline) ((grove math semantic) #:prefix ◆:)]
@title{Differentiate a polynomial, then simplify}
@summary{Two separate passes with independently replayable provenance.}

@(define polynomial @◆:expression{x^3 + 2*x})
@(define derivative-report (math-differentiate polynomial "x" #:assumptions '("scalar(x)")))
@(define derivative (math-report-result derivative-report))

For a polynomial in independent scalar coordinates, Grove supports exact integer and rational constants, variables, sums, differences, products and natural-number powers. Other declared coordinates are held constant. The first pass expands differentiation rules; the second computes constants, combines identical scalar terms and removes neutral factors, zero products and trivial powers. It does not expand products into a canonical polynomial.

@with-assumptions["scalar(x)"]{
@heading{Automatic differentiation in two passes}
@verbatim["@(define polynomial @◆:expression{x^3 + 2*x})\n@differentiate[\"x\" polynomial]"]
@math[polynomial]
@differentiate["x" polynomial]

By default the article shows the starting expression and the result. Open the steps to inspect the raw differentiation pass and the following simplification pass, with readable rule descriptions. All expressions render as native MathML, including the derivative operator and fractions.

@heading{Showing only the endpoints}
@differentiate["x" polynomial #:view 'result]

The result view omits the step display; it uses the same checked certificate.

@heading{Reuse a computed value in an authored chain}
@check-derive[#:view 'full]{
  @◆:derivative[polynomial "x"]
  differentiate(x^3,x) + differentiate(2*x,x)
  3*x^2*differentiate(x,x) + differentiate(2*x,x)
  3*x^2*1 + differentiate(2*x,x)
  3*x^2 + differentiate(2*x,x)
  3*x^2 + (differentiate(2,x)*x + 2*differentiate(x,x))
  3*x^2 + (0*x + 2*differentiate(x,x))
  3*x^2 + (0 + 2*differentiate(x,x))
  3*x^2 + 2*differentiate(x,x)
  3*x^2 + 2*1
  @|derivative|
}

The endpoints reuse the original value and its computed result; intermediate
steps are chosen by the author. Each adjacent pair is checked as one transition.
Generated operands retain their input locations across both passes. Step labels
expose those locations on hover, and the result remains a normal Semantic value.

@heading{A composite base and a constant coordinate}
@with-assumptions["scalar(y)"]{
@differentiate["x"]{(x+y)^3}
}

The power rule differentiates its base as well. Since y is an independent coordinate, its derivative with respect to x is zero. Parentheses around a sum used as a power base are preserved during rendering.

@heading{Exact coefficients and repeated terms}
@differentiate["x"]{(1/2)*x^2 + x*x}

Rational coefficients remain exact. The product rule produces two copies of x; collection and coefficient calculation reduce the final expression to 3*x.

@heading{Search from a derivative term to a target}
@search-derive["3*x^2"]{differentiate(x^3,x)}

The symbolic derivative is an ordinary mathematical term. Goal search can therefore combine differentiation and arithmetic rules; the convenience command above keeps those operations in separate passes instead.

@heading{Verify selected steps by hand}
@check-derive[#:view 'full]{
  differentiate(x^3,x)
  3*x^2*differentiate(x,x)
  3*x^2*1
  3*x^2
}
}

@heading{Reject an incorrect result}
@check-derive[#:assumptions "scalar(x)" #:on-error 'show]{
  differentiate(x,x)
  2
}

A claimed derivative of 2 fails replay against the variable rule. The automatic polynomial pass also rejects nonconstant division, functions such as sin(x), and negative or symbolic exponents before producing a partial derivation. Bounds limit expansion size and step count; this is not an integration engine or a general computer algebra system.

@heading{Inspect an unfinished calculation}
@differentiate["x" #:assumptions "scalar(x)" #:max-steps 1 #:on-error 'show]{x^3}

The report preserves the first power-rule step and marks its step limit. Scheme code can inspect that partial certificate; the document command refuses to display it as a completed derivative.

The same certificate machinery checks @link["vector-analysis"]{vector identities}.
